#### Zero Degree Calorimeter

Zero Degree Calorimeters (ZDCs) [108] are hadronic tungsten calorimeters used for detecting primarily neutrons. Two ZDC detectors are located 18 m away, past the DX magnets, on either side from the interaction point as shown in Figure 2.13. The DX magnets bend the charged particles allowing mostly neutral charged particles to hit the ZDC detectors. The ZDC detectors sample with a transverse area of 10\(\times\)13.6 \(cm^{2}\) with respect to the beam. ZDCs are composed of three modules made from alternating layers of tungsten absorber and Cerenkov fiber ribbons placed 45\({}^{\circ}\) relative to the incident beam. The fiber angle optimize the collection of Cerenkov light produced from the secondary showers [109]. A Shower Maximum Detector (SMD) resides between the first and second ZDC module. The SMD is composed of eight horizontal and seven vertical slats. Each horizontal(vertical) slat comprises of four(three) fiber

