### 4.3 Particle Identification

#### Using TPC

The Time Projection Chamber (TPC) is the main tracking detector in the STAR experiment. It is capable of measuring charged particles within \(\mid\eta\mid\)\(<\) 1.8 and within full azimuthal coverage [200]. TPC operates within a constant magnetic field and hence it can measure the momentum of the charged tracks following their curvature. In this analysis, \(\phi\)-meson are measured through the decay channel \(\phi\)\(\rightarrow\)\(K^{+}\)\(+\)\(K^{-}\). Kaons are identified by their ionization energy loss as a function of momentum inside the TPC gas volume. The mean specific energy loss in the TPC for different particles as a function of rigidity (charge\(\times\)momentum) are shown in Figure 4.3. In this figure, the theoretical predictions of energy loss for the charged particles are also shown in solid lines. The theoretical values of specific energy loss are obtained from Bichsel

Figure 4.2: _The multiplicity distribution of charged particles in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s}_{NN}\) = 200 GeV_function [201]. The Bichsel function is used to determine the n\(\sigma\) and is defined as

\[n\sigma=\frac{1}{R}\times\log[\frac{dE/dx_{measured}}{dE/dx_{theory}}] \tag{4.2}\]

where dE/dx is the specific ionization energy loss per unit path length and R is the dE/dx resolution. The kaons are shown in black text. The track quality cuts for kaons selection using TPC are listed in the Table 4.2. In order to ensure good track momentum reconstruction, short tracks were eliminated from the analysis by requiring all tracks to have \(p_{T}>\) 0.15 GeV/c and a minimum number of 15 fit points (nHitsFit) in TPC for each track. The effect of track-splitting due to the tracking algorithm is minimized by further requiring that the number of fit points is more than half of the number of total possible hit points for a track i.e. nHitsFit/Max.nHitsFit \(\geq\) 0.52. In addition, all tracks with distance of closest approach (DCA) from primary vertex greater than 3.0 cm were removed to reject the tracks coming from sources other than primary vertex. Finally kaon tracks with \(\eta<\) 1.0, where the acceptance

Figure 4.3: _The mean specific energy loss(dE/dx) as a function of rigidity (p*q) in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s}_{NN}\) = 200 GeV_

of TPC is uniform, has been used in the analysis.

#### Using ToF

The ToF detector measures the time (\(\tau\)) taken by a track to traverse the distance (L) from the primary vertex to the ToF. The ToF has full azimuthal coverage and works within \(\mid\eta\mid\)\(<\) 0.9 [202]. Using the time (\(\tau\)), we can calulate the velocity (\(\beta\)) of the track as \(\beta\) = L/(c\(\tau\)), where c is velocity of light. Using \(\tau\) from ToF and corresponding momentum (p) from TPC, we calculate the mass (m) of the charged tracks using the relativistic equation:

\[m^{2}=p^{2}(\frac{1}{\beta^{2}}-1) \tag{4.3}\]

Therefore using this \(m^{2}\), we can enhance the identification of the tracks in higher \(p_{T}\)(\(>\)1.0 GeV/c). The mass squared (\(m^{2}\)) as a function of momentum is shown in Figure 4.4. The \(m^{2}\) cut which are applied for this analysis are also shown in Table 4.2.

