#### \(\phi\) - binning method

In this \(\phi\) - binning method [245] one has to measure the raw yield of the chosen particle as function of angle (\(\phi\) - \(\psi_{n}\)), where \(\phi\) is the azimuthal angle of the particle in laboratory-frame and \(\psi_{n}\) is the event plane angle. The extracted yield as a function (\(\phi\) - \(\psi_{n}\)) can then be fitted by the following function to get the observed \(v_{n}\)

\[\frac{dN}{d(\phi-\psi}=p_{0}[1+2v_{n}\cos(n(\phi-\psi_{n}))] \tag{6.12}\]

Where \(p_{0}\) and \(v_{n}\) are the parameters. In case of resonances, where the signal typically consists of a very small mass peak sitting above a large combinatorial background. It is difficult to extract the raw yields accurately. Again, the (\(\phi\) - \(\psi_{n}\)) bin method of extracting \(v_{n}\) requires that for each \(p_{T}\) bin, the small signal is further divided into bins of (\(\phi\) - \(\psi_{n}\)) angle. This can lead to a large systematic errors in the final \(v_{n}\) measurement as the systematic (or statistical) error in extracting the raw yield is large. Because of this reason, another method (\(v_{n}\) vs \(m_{inv}\)), described in the following section is used to extract the resonance \(v_{n}\) co-efficient.

#### \(v_{n}\) vs \(m_{inv}\) method

To extract \(v_{n}\) of \(\phi\)-meson, \(v_{n}\) vs \(m_{inv}\). [248] method is used in the present analysis. For single particle, the probability distribution of the azimuthal angle \(\phi\) (measured with respect to a fixed direction in the laboratory) can be written as:

\[p(\phi-\psi_{r})=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sum_{n=+\infty}^{-\infty}v_{n}e^{in(\phi-\psi_ {r})} \tag{6.13}\]where \(\psi_{r}\) is the reaction plane angle. If the system is symmetric w.r.t the reaction plane with real \(v_{n}\), Eq. 6.13 reduces to:

\[p(\phi-\psi_{r})=\frac{1}{2\pi}(1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}v_{n}\cos(n(\phi-\psi_{R}))) \tag{6.14}\]

For pairs of particles, Eq. 6.13. can be extended as:

\[p(\phi_{pair}-\psi_{r})=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sum_{n=+\infty}^{-\infty}v_{n}^{pair}e^ {in(\phi_{pair}-\psi_{r})} \tag{6.15}\]

where \(\phi\) has been replaced with \(\phi_{pair}\). For resonance particle (e.g. \(\phi\)-meson etc.) which decays to two daughter particles (e.g. \(\phi\to K^{+}K^{-}\)), \(\phi_{pair}\) is the azimuthal angle of the parent resonance particle. In Eq. 6.15, \(v_{n}^{pair}\) are called "\(pair-flow^{\prime\prime}\) coefficients defined by \(v_{n}^{pair}\)=\(<e^{-in(\phi_{pair}-\phi_{R}}>\), with the normalization \(v_{0}^{pair}\)=1. Since the probability distribution is real-valued so the coefficients also satisfy the property

\[v_{-n}^{pair}=(v_{n}^{pair})^{*} \tag{6.16}\]

But unlike the single-particle flow \(v_{n}\), the pair-flow coefficient \(v_{n}^{pair}\) is in general not a real number. As a consequence, sine terms are also present in the real form of Fourier expansion and Eq. 6.15 is replaced by

\[p(\phi-\psi_{r})=\frac{1}{2\pi}(1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}v_{c,n}^{pair}\cos(n( \phi-\psi_{r}))+(v_{s,n}^{pair}\sin(n(\phi-\psi_{r})))) \tag{6.17}\]

where the real coefficients \(v_{c,n}^{pair}=<\cos(n(\phi-\psi_{r}))>\) and \(v_{s,n}^{pair}=\sin(n(\phi-\psi_{r}))\) are related to the complex \(v_{n}^{pair}\) by the relation \(v_{n}^{pair}=v_{c,n}^{pair}-iv_{s,n}^{pair}\).

For a resonance particle which is identified through its mass peak in an invariant mass distribution consisting of all combinations of candidate decay daughter particle pairs, the invariant mass distribution can be separated into:

\[N_{pairs}(m_{inv})=N_{b}(m_{inv})+N_{s}(m_{(inv)} \tag{6.18}\]

where \(N_{b}\) is the number of combinatorial background pairs and \(N_{s}\) is the number of signal pairs. In similar way, for the \(\phi\)-meson so we can write the contributions to the pair-flow coefficients as:

\[N_{pairs}(m_{inv})v_{c,n}(m_{inv})=N_{b}(m_{inv})v_{c,n}^{b}(m_{inv})+N_{\phi,n}(m _{inv})v_{c,n}^{\phi}) \tag{6.19}\]

\[N_{pairs}(m_{inv})v_{s,n}(m_{inv})=N_{b}(m_{inv})v_{s,n}^{b}(m_{inv})+N_{\phi,n}( m_{inv})v_{s,n}^{\phi}) \tag{6.20}\]

Symmetry with respect to the reaction plane for \(\phi\) particles implies that \(v_{s,n}^{\phi}=0\), further if the background is composed of uncorrelated particles, then \(v_{s,n}^{b}=0\). The \(v_{n}\) distribution as a function of \(m_{inv}\) can then be fitted using the following equation(based on Eq. 6.19):

\[v_{n}^{S+B}=v_{n}^{S}\frac{S}{S+B}(m_{inv})+v_{n}^{B}\frac{B}{S+B}(m_{inv}) \tag{6.21}\]

where \(S\) is the signal yield, \(B\) is background yield, \(v_{n}^{S}\), \(v_{n}^{B}\) and \(v_{n}^{S+B}\) are the \(v_{n}\) of signal, background and total particles, respectively. The ratios \(\frac{S}{S+B}\) and \(\frac{B}{S+B}\) are functions of invariant mass. The term \(v_{n}^{B}(m_{inv})\) is parametrized as a linear function in order to take care of the \(v_{n}^{B}\) value as a function of (\(m_{inv}\)). Here \(v_{n}^{B}\) has been taken

Figure 6.5: \(v_{n}\) _as a function of invariant mass. The red line is the fit to the data points._

as a \(3^{rd}\) order polynomial function of invariant mass. The fit result \(v_{n}^{S}\) is the final \(v_{n}\). The each term of Eq. 6.21 is shown in Figure 6.5 for \(\phi\)-meson in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s}_{NN}\) = 200 GeV.

